  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Wellness Survey Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a wellness survey?\", \"a\": \"A wellness survey asks people how they are doing across areas of health and daily life, such as sleep, stress, money, and relationships. Employers run them most often, and colleges and gyms use them too. Results are read by group, such as a site or a class year, to show where help is missing.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you create an employee wellness survey?\", \"a\": \"Describe it to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder'>AI survey builder</a>: who answers, which areas to cover, and the smallest group allowed to see its own results. It drafts the questions plus a results view by team or site, and it is free to start. Pilot the survey with one team before the whole company gets it.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are some good survey questions for a wellness program?\", \"a\": \"Good ones ask about the recent past and cover one thing at a time. One asks how often in the past two weeks you woke up rested. Another asks whether you could cover a $1,000 emergency expense without borrowing. Wellness survey questions written this way give an honest baseline, and an open comment box in each area catches what a rating scale misses.\"}, {\"q\": \"What areas should an employee wellbeing survey include?\", \"a\": \"Most cover four or five areas: physical health, emotional health, financial security, social connection, and the effect of work itself. SAMHSA's model of wellness names eight dimensions, and few employers measure all of them. People often use wellness and wellbeing interchangeably, though wellness usually points to physical health and wellbeing to the wider picture.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many questions should a wellness survey have?\", \"a\": \"About 15 to 20 works for an annual survey, which most people finish in around five minutes. That leaves room for three or four questions per area and an open box or two. A check-in repeated every term or quarter can stay at five or six questions and still show a trend.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is there a free wellness survey template?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Emergent writes a wellness survey template from a few lines of description, and you can start for free. Say which areas to cover and who sees results, then edit the draft questions. Answers land in a database you own, with no cap on how many come in.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are some wellness survey examples?\", \"a\": \"National examples include EBRI's annual Workplace Wellness Survey of US workers and Syracuse University's National Wellbeing Survey of adults aged 18 to 64. Colleges run the ACHA's National College Health Assessment. A smaller one is a gym's day-30 member check-in, which asks about progress where a customer satisfaction survey asks about service.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a wellness form?\", \"a\": \"A wellness form usually collects one person's health details for a specific purpose, such as a gym intake or a clinic visit, and someone reads it individually. A wellness survey asks many people the same questions and is read in aggregate. A studio might use the form at signup and the survey at day 30.\"}, {\"q\": \"Should employee wellness survey questions be anonymous?\", \"a\": \"Confidential is usually the better choice. A confidential survey knows who answered, which allows reminders and an opt-in follow-up, while reports hide any group below a minimum such as five answers. An anonymous survey stores no identity at all, which rules out both. On Emergent you set the minimum yourself, and responses are stored in your own database.\"}, {\"q\": \"How often should you send wellness surveys?\", \"a\": \"Once a year suits a full survey, timed a few months before benefits or budgets are set. Many teams add a short check-in each quarter between rounds. When people are already tired of surveys, a small wellbeing section inside the annual <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/employee-engagement-survey'>employee engagement survey</a> can stand in for a separate round.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
